@@ -19,3 +19,20 @@ a `~` not followed by `0` or `1`), and when it resolves to nothing: a missing me
 non-canonical array index (`01`, `-1`), the `-` token, or a step into a primitive. Inherited properties
 (`/constructor`, `/__proto__` on a plain object) are never resolved: only own members are, so
 untrusted pointers cannot reach prototypes. Run the tests with `npm test`.
+
+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, applies an array of operations (`add`,
+`remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`) and returns the patched document as a new value.
+
+```ts
+applyPatch({ foo: "bar" }, [{ op: "add", path: "/baz", value: "qux" }]); // { foo: "bar", baz: "qux" }
+```
+
+Neither the document nor the patch is mutated, and the result shares no structure with them. It throws an
+`Error` when the patch is not an array, an operation is malformed (unknown `op`, missing `path`, `value`
+or `from`, invalid pointer), or an operation fails: a missing target, an out-of-range array index, a
+move into the value's own child, or a `test` that does not match (deep equality, member order ignored).
+A failing patch has no partial effect. Removing the whole document (`remove` with path `""`) is rejected,
+as no JSON value would remain. Unrecognized members of an operation are ignored. Paths follow the same
+pointer rules as `resolvePointer`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary member and prototypes are never reached.
